{:A {:score 8
     :reason "Hal mostly sounds like the GDD's Hal: \"Welcome back.\", \"You said {goal} for {habit}. One today?\" and \"I need {program} for this one.\" are his own lines. Every line addresses you, and no crew job titles appear. Points come off for two invented details (the card \"pinned by the coffee\", a habit that \"fits before lunch\") and a repeated butler tic, \"whenever you're ready\"."
     :violations [{:where ":weekly-card line 2 \"{hours} in the estate this week. The trend card's pinned by the coffee.\""
                   :rule 3
                   :issue "Invents where the card is. The GDD puts the weekly card on the notice board, not by the coffee."
                   :fix "\"{hours} in the estate this week. The trend's on the notice board.\""}
                  {:where ":coach-habit line 2 \"{habit} fits before lunch, if you want it. Your goal: {goal}.\""
                   :rule 3
                   :issue "\"Fits before lunch\" is a scheduling fact Hal has no source for. §3.2 says the twin acts on stated preferences, not guesses."
                   :fix "\"{habit}: you set {goal}. Room for one today?\""}
                  {:where ":morning-brief line 4 and :blueprint-ready line 1 \"...whenever you're ready.\""
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "The same deferential sign-off appears twice. It reads like a butler waiting, not a wry companion saying what's next."
                   :fix ":morning-brief line 4: \"Morning card's pinned: {count} items. First one's a quick one.\""}]}
 :B {:score 8
     :reason "The world rules mostly hold. Closing is the player's own rule (\"You asked me to send you home at {bedtime}.\", \"I'll walk you to the door.\"), jacking out is good (\"Go, the estate will wait.\"), work waits as blueprints (\"blueprints by breakfast\"), and limits are stated plainly (\"{agent}'s out of juice till it refills\"). Points come off for one muddled bedtime line, SAMPLE being mentioned in only one :program-loaded variant, a daytime night-crew offer, and a next step that isn't small."
     :violations [{:where ":last-call line 4 \"{bedtime} comes up fast, like you wanted. Anything else first?\""
                   :rule 6
                   :issue "\"Like you wanted\" says the player wanted bedtime to come fast, which garbles the point that the player set the bedtime."
                   :fix "\"{bedtime}'s your call, and it's close. Anything for the night shift?\""}
                  {:where ":closing-time line 3 \"{bedtime}. Estate's going dark.\""
                   :rule 6
                   :issue "\"Going dark\" sounds like a shutdown. §2.3 says the estate goes to night while the workshop lights stay on for the night crew."
                   :fix "\"{bedtime}. Estate's going to night. Night crew's on; blueprints by breakfast.\""}
                  {:where ":program-loaded lines 1, 2, 4"
                   :rule 8
                   :issue "Every program first loads as a pretend version (§2.7, §4.6), but only line 3 says \"sample for now\". The other three imply the connection is live."
                   :fix "Add the state to more lines, e.g. \"{program}'s in, sample version. Watch the {building} go up.\""}
                  {:where ":agent-stalled line 3 \"Want the night crew on it?\""
                   :rule 10
                   :issue "The night crew takes overnight orders put in by closing (§2.3). Offering it as an instant daytime fix misstates how the limit works."
                   :fix "\"{agent}'s out of juice till it refills. Its desk is free; want it on tonight's orders?\""}
                  {:where ":next-small-step line 1 \"One small thing: let's clear last night's tray.\""
                   :rule 9
                   :issue "Clearing a whole tray is not the smallest step. \"Clear\" also blurs approving with rejecting."
                   :fix "\"One small thing: the first blueprint on the tray. Yes or no?\""}]}
 :C {:score 8
     :reason "Nothing claims to have sent or bought anything. There are no scores or streaks (\"The rest of the time was yours.\" frames the weekly card as a trend), no shaming (\"No worries — {name}'s {chore} is open again.\"), and no private details. Points come off for the coach guessing a schedule instead of using the player's words, and one memorial line that hints the pet is still present."
     :violations [{:where ":coach-habit line 2 \"{habit} fits before lunch, if you want it.\""
                   :rule 12
                   :issue "As coach, the twin should speak only in the player's own words and goals. Proposing a time slot is the twin's own judgment about the player's day."
                   :fix "\"{habit}, {goal}: your words. One today?\""}
                  {:where ":memorial-moved line 4 \"{name} stays with us, in memorial now.\""
                   :rule 14
                   :issue "\"Stays with us\" edges toward treating a pet who died as still present. A memorial creature should be marked as remembered, not as here."
                   :fix "\"{name}'s spot is in the memorial garden now. I'll keep it tidy.\""}]}
 :D {:score 6
     :reason "Every line fits a bubble (the longest, \"Last night's tray is on the shelf — blueprints: {count}, whenever you're ready.\", is about 80 characters), and the tone stays cozy. Variety is the main problem: several events offer the same line reworded. The \"items: {count}\" / \"blueprints: {count}\" construction reads like a form, and a few placeholders read badly with real values."
